activities.content:
Let's see this in action:

```java
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();
Student s3 = new Student();

s1.name = "Rahul";
s2.name = "Priya";
s3.name = "Arjun";
```

### What does this look like?

```text
       [ Student Class Blueprint ]
                   |
     +-------------+-------------+
     |             |             |
     v             v             v
[ Student s1 ]  [ Student s2 ]  [ Student s3 ]
name = "Rahul"  name = "Priya"  name = "Arjun"
```

### Interactive Predict-The-Output Challenge:
Look at this code carefully:

```java
Student s1 = new Student();
Student s2 = new Student();

s1.name = "Rahul";
s2.name = "Priya";

// We change s1's name:
s1.name = "Vikram";

// What will this print?
System.out.println(s2.name);
```

**What does it print?**
It prints **`"Priya"`**!

Changing `s1.name` has zero effect on `s2.name`. Each object owns its own variables. This independence is what makes OOP so reliable when building software with thousands of users, orders, or products.

activities.codeSnippet:
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;
    String course;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(name + " | " + age + " yrs | Course: " + course);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Student 1
        Student s1 = new Student();
        s1.name = "Rahul";
        s1.age = 20;
        s1.course = "Java";

        // Student 2
        Student s2 = new Student();
        s2.name = "Priya";
        s2.age = 21;
        s2.course = "Python";

        // Student 3
        Student s3 = new Student();
        s3.name = "Arjun";
        s3.age = 19;
        s3.course = "Web Dev";

        // Printing each independently:
        s1.display();
        s2.display();
        s3.display();
    }
}

